From fa6ec16e2e5f6b01c9a530161ef1f516a7b32869 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 14:38:30 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(kanban): don't trust backfilled UUIDv7 uids as event time
 [daveey]

_event_order (#2946) took any UUIDv7 uid's milliseconds as the event's
time. kanban_db_sync backfills uid-less rows with a v7 uid stamped at
backfill time, so on the hub default replica 4055 of 21174 relevant
events carry a uid whose second disagrees with created_at. A backfilled
uid on an older operator unblock could outrank a newer shared-blocker
assertion (release fails open for one probe), or a backfilled uid on an
older assertion could void a newer release (fail closed).

Use the uid milliseconds only when they fall in the row's created_at
second; otherwise order by created_at * 1000. Local ids still break
exact ties. The consistency check is now one helper,
consistent_uid_milliseconds, shared with the fence's _uid_before_check
(behaviour of that check unchanged).

Two existing fixtures deliberately set created_at against the uid to
prove the uid wins; they now keep the uid inside created_at's second.

Proof: RED (old code, new tests): 8 failed = every case of
test_backfilled_uid_orders_by_created_at (older unblock with later
backfilled uid stays held; older assertion with later backfilled uid
releases; other side v7 or uid-less; own_only both). GREEN: 28 test
files importing the shared-blocker modules, 719 tests pass.
Follow-up of review t_01a1da6fa75e36d0 on PR #2946 (nit 1).
